From 9b27c46351d3fb2bf4da750803a379c6a4e2563a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:23:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11084/11171] Supersede a five-hour-old "not built" list that
 names three built things

The handoff block written at 12:00Z lists four filed-and-unbuilt items. Three
were built by 16:41Z: the cascade check, the induction_attempts wiring, and the
exp6968 sentinel message. The fourth, marker-scan scoping, is largely moot since
the conductor's own 10:54Z fix cleared the case that motivated it. Two further
checks exist that were not on the list because they did not exist to be filed.

Stale in the worst direction: it tells a successor to build things that already
work.

Appended rather than edited per never-prune, with a pointer to which block is
current. The operator decisions in the superseded block are unchanged and were
deliberately not touched.

Records the pattern, since this is the second time today status.md described a
state that had already moved -- the first being the induce run still described as
in flight eight hours after it finished. A hand-written not-built list rots faster
than anything else in a handoff because build state changes without anyone
revisiting the prose. Build state is derivable, operator decisions are not, and
only the second kind belongs in prose. Not restructuring the format here; that is
more than a status append.

Both staleness cases were caught by the hourly what-is-not-written pass rather
than by anything mechanical, which is an argument for the pass and against
trusting the document.

